nodecode-deadbolt is a rewrite of seanebones-lang's seanebones-lang/deadbolt-hermes-plugin:

  https://github.com/seanebones-lang/deadbolt-hermes-plugin
  commit 177fff79a8b6308f8d182146ed8a6e21dc84c565
  Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 NextEleven LLC (LICENSE)

The plugin registers two tools — deadbolt_prepare_write and
deadbolt_commit_write — that make an agent propose one new text file and a
person approve it before it exists. This rewrite keeps that whole route: the
two tool names and their wording, the request envelope field for field
(version, agent_id, tool, dest, arguments, nonce, expires_at, with the
arguments' workspace, path and content), the 32-hex nonce and the five-minute
deadline, the 32768-byte cap on an envelope, the relative-path rules, the
review file written 0600 and never overwritten, the one outbound call to
http://127.0.0.1:<port>/admit-action with the X-Deadbolt-Admission credential
and no redirect, the decision codes, the refusal to write on a denial, the
admitted-but-unknown outcome after a filesystem failure, and the tool text
that tells the model to stop rather than approve itself. It is Common Lisp
against Nodecode's cell contract instead of a Python plugin: the tools are
registered by one NLE:DEFINE-CELL declaration, the same verbs are callable as
deadbolt:prepare and deadbolt:commit through eval, and /deadbolt reports where
the settings stand.

Left out: the harness plumbing — ctx.register_tool, ctx.get_config, and the
active profile's secret read through agent.secret_scope.get_secret, since a
Nodecode cell declares its own section and reads this run's credential from
it. Also left out: plugins.plugin_storage's plugin_data_dir, replaced by the
cell's own review directory; the plugin's strict duplicate-JSON-key refusal
on the review file; the POSIX directory-descriptor walk (O_EXCL and
O_NOFOLLOW still carry it on SBCL, with a plain exclusive open elsewhere, so
the Windows refusal is a fallback rather than an error); and the
acceptance-test harness of the original. Preparation still persists after a
commit or an expiry, as the plugin's disclosure says.
